🟢 PROBLEM #1: COUNT ALL SUBSTRINGS (EASY)
===============================================================================

📋 PROBLEM STATEMENT:
Given a string, count the total number of substrings possible.
Input: "abc"
Output: 6
Explanation: Substrings are "a", "b", "c", "ab", "bc", "abc"

💡 INTUITION:
- For a string of length n, every character can be the start of a substring
- From each starting position i, we can form substrings ending at positions i, i+1, ..., n-1
- This gives us a mathematical formula: n*(n+1)/2

🔍 APPROACH:
1. Brute Force: Generate all substrings and count them - O(n³)
2. Mathematical: Use formula n*(n+1)/2 - O(1)
3. Iterative: Count substrings starting from each position - O(n²)

⚡ COMPLEXITY:
Time: O(1) for mathematical approach, O(n²) for iterative
Space: O(1)

💻 JAVA SOLUTION:
```java
public class CountAllSubstrings {
    
    // Method 1: Mathematical Formula (Most Efficient)
    public static int countSubstringsMath(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int n = s.length();
        return n * (n + 1) / 2;
    }
    
    // Method 2: Iterative Approach (Educational)
    public static int countSubstringsIterative(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        // For each starting position
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // Count substrings starting at position i
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                count++;
                // Optional: Print substring for visualization
                // System.out.println(s.substring(i, j + 1));
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String test = "abc";
        System.out.println("Mathematical: " + countSubstringsMath(test));
        System.out.println("Iterative: " + countSubstringsIterative(test));
    }
}
```

🔥 HINTS:
1. Think about what defines a unique substring (start and end positions)
2. For string of length 1: 1 substring, length 2: 3 substrings, length 3: 6 substrings
3. Pattern: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15... (triangular numbers)

