🟡 PROBLEM #2: COUNT SUBSTRINGS WITH UNIQUE CHARACTERS (EASY-MEDIUM)
===============================================================================

📋 PROBLEM STATEMENT:
Count the number of substrings that contain only unique characters.
Input: "abcabc"
Output: 10
Explanation: "a", "b", "c", "ab", "bc", "ca", "abc", "bca", "cab", "a", "b", "c"

💡 INTUITION:
- We need to find all substrings where no character repeats
- Use sliding window technique to maintain unique character window
- When we find a duplicate, we need to shrink the window from left

🔍 APPROACH:
1. Brute Force: Check each substring for uniqueness - O(n³)
2. Sliding Window: Expand and contract window based on duplicates - O(n²)
3. Optimized Sliding Window: Use HashMap to track last occurrence - O(n)

⚡ COMPLEXITY:
Time: O(n) for optimized approach
Space: O(min(m,n)) where m is character set size

💻 JAVA SOLUTION:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class CountUniqueSubstrings {
    
    public static int countUniqueSubstrings(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        // For each starting position
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            Set<Character> seen = new HashSet<>();
            
            // Extend substring from position i
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                char c = s.charAt(j);
                
                // If character already seen, break
                if (seen.contains(c)) {
                    break;
                }
                
                seen.add(c);
                count++;
                // Optional: Print unique substring
                // System.out.println(s.substring(i, j + 1));
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    // Optimized approach using sliding window
    public static int countUniqueSubstringsOptimized(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            boolean[] visited = new boolean[256]; // ASCII characters
            
            for (int j = i; j < n && !visited[s.charAt(j)]; j++) {
                visited[s.charAt(j)] = true;
                count++;
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String test = "abcabc";
        System.out.println("Count: " + countUniqueSubstrings(test));
        System.out.println("Optimized: " + countUniqueSubstringsOptimized(test));
    }
}
```

🔥 HINTS:
1. Use HashSet to track characters in current substring
2. When you encounter a duplicate, start a new substring from the next position
3. Think about when to break the inner loop

===============================================================================
🟡 PROBLEM #3: COUNT PALINDROMIC SUBSTRINGS (MEDIUM)
===============================================================================

📋 PROBLEM STATEMENT:
Count the number of palindromic substrings in a given string.
Input: "aaa"
Output: 6
Explanation: "a", "a", "a", "aa", "aa", "aaa"

💡 INTUITION:
- A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards
- We can expand around each possible center (character and between characters)
- For each center, expand outward while characters match

🔍 APPROACH:
1. Brute Force: Check each substring for palindrome property - O(n³)
2. Expand Around Centers: For each center, expand while matching - O(n²)
3. Dynamic Programming: Build solution using smaller palindromes - O(n²)

⚡ COMPLEXITY:
Time: O(n²) for expand around centers
Space: O(1) for expand around centers, O(n²) for DP

💻 JAVA SOLUTION:
```java
public class CountPalindromicSubstrings {
    
    public static int countPalindromicSubstrings(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        // Check for palindromes centered at each position and between positions
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // Odd length palindromes (center at i)
            count += expandAroundCenter(s, i, i);
            
            // Even length palindromes (center between i and i+1)
            count += expandAroundCenter(s, i, i + 1);
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    private static int expandAroundCenter(String s, int left, int right) {
        int count = 0;
        
        // Expand while characters match and indices are valid
        while (left >= 0 && right < s.length() && s.charAt(left) == s.charAt(right)) {
            count++;
            left--;
            right++;
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    // Alternative: Dynamic Programming approach
    public static int countPalindromicSubstringsDP(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n][n];
        int count = 0;
        
        // Single characters are palindromes
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i][i] = true;
            count++;
        }
        
        // Check for 2-character palindromes
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(i + 1)) {
                dp[i][i + 1] = true;
                count++;
            }
        }
        
        // Check for palindromes of length 3 and more
        for (int length = 3; length <= n; length++) {
            for (int i = 0; i <= n - length; i++) {
                int j = i + length - 1;
                
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j) && dp[i + 1][j - 1]) {
                    dp[i][j] = true;
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String test = "aaa";
        System.out.println("Expand Around Centers: " + countPalindromicSubstrings(test));
        System.out.println("Dynamic Programming: " + countPalindromicSubstringsDP(test));
        
        // Test with more complex string
        String test2 = "abc";
        System.out.println("Test 'abc': " + countPalindromicSubstrings(test2)); // Should be 3
    }
}
```

🔥 HINTS:
1. Every single character is a palindrome
2. For each position, consider it as center of odd-length palindrome
3. For each adjacent pair, consider them as center of even-length palindrome
4. Expand outward while characters match

