🟠 PROBLEM #4: COUNT SUBSTRINGS WITH K DISTINCT CHARACTERS (MEDIUM)
===============================================================================

📋 PROBLEM STATEMENT:
Count the number of substrings that have exactly K distinct characters.
Input: s = "aba", k = 2
Output: 2
Explanation: Substrings with exactly 2 distinct characters: "ab", "ba"

💡 INTUITION:
- This is a classic sliding window problem
- We need to count substrings with exactly K distinct characters
- Use the fact that: exactly K = at most K - at most (K-1)

🔍 APPROACH:
1. Brute Force: Check each substring for distinct character count - O(n³)
2. Sliding Window Helper: Count substrings with at most K distinct chars - O(n)
3. Final Answer: atMostK(K) - atMostK(K-1) - O(n)

⚡ COMPLEXITY:
Time: O(n) where n is string length
Space: O(K) for storing character frequencies

💻 JAVA SOLUTION:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class CountSubstringsKDistinct {
    
    public static int countSubstringsExactlyK(String s, int k) {
        if (k == 0) return 0;
        return countSubstringsAtMostK(s, k) - countSubstringsAtMostK(s, k - 1);
    }
    
    private static int countSubstringsAtMostK(String s, int k) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0 || k <= 0) return 0;
        
        Map<Character, Integer> charCount = new HashMap<>();
        int left = 0, count = 0;
        
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char rightChar = s.charAt(right);
            charCount.put(rightChar, charCount.getOrDefault(rightChar, 0) + 1);
            
            // Shrink window if we have more than k distinct characters
            while (charCount.size() > k) {
                char leftChar = s.charAt(left);
                charCount.put(leftChar, charCount.get(leftChar) - 1);
                
                if (charCount.get(leftChar) == 0) {
                    charCount.remove(leftChar);
                }
                left++;
            }
            
            // Add all substrings ending at 'right' with at most k distinct chars
            count += right - left + 1;
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    // Alternative: Direct approach (less efficient but more intuitive)
    public static int countSubstringsExactlyKDirect(String s, int k) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        // Check all possible substrings
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            Set<Character> distinctChars = new HashSet<>();
            
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                distinctChars.add(s.charAt(j));
                
                // If exactly k distinct characters
                if (distinctChars.size() == k) {
                    count++;
                } else if (distinctChars.size() > k) {
                    // No point continuing from this starting position
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String test = "aba";
        int k = 2;
        
        System.out.println("Sliding Window: " + countSubstringsExactlyK(test, k));
        System.out.println("Direct Approach: " + countSubstringsExactlyKDirect(test, k));
        
        // Additional test cases
        System.out.println("Test 'aabab' with k=2: " + countSubstringsExactlyK("aabab", 2));
        System.out.println("Test 'abcabc' with k=3: " + countSubstringsExactlyK("abcabc", 3));
    }
}
```

🔥 HINTS:
1. Think about using the sliding window technique
2. Key insight: exactly K = at most K - at most (K-1)
3. Use HashMap to count frequency of characters in current window
4. When window has > k distinct chars, shrink from left

