🔴 PROBLEM #5: NUMBER OF SUBSTRINGS CONTAINING ALL THREE CHARACTERS (MEDIUM)
===============================================================================

📋 PROBLEM STATEMENT:
Given a string s consisting only of characters 'a', 'b', and 'c', return the 
number of substrings containing at least one occurrence of all three characters.

Input: s = "abcabc"
Output: 10
Explanation: Substrings containing all three characters: "abc", "abca", "abcab", 
"abcabc", "bca", "bcab", "bcabc", "cab", "cabc", "abc"

💡 INTUITION:
- We need substrings that contain at least one 'a', one 'b', and one 'c'
- Use sliding window to find the smallest valid window, then count all extensions
- Key insight: If we have a valid substring from i to j, then all substrings 
  from i to k (where k >= j) are also valid

🔍 APPROACH:
1. Brute Force: Check each substring for all three characters - O(n³)
2. Sliding Window: Find leftmost valid position for each right pointer - O(n²)
3. Optimized Sliding Window: Track last positions of each character - O(n)

⚡ COMPLEXITY:
Time: O(n) for optimized approach
Space: O(1) - only storing indices for 3 characters

💻 JAVA SOLUTION:
```java
public class SubstringsWithAllThreeChars {
    
    // Method 1: Optimized Sliding Window (Most Efficient)
    public static int numberOfSubstrings(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() < 3) return 0;
        
        // Track last seen positions of 'a', 'b', 'c'
        int[] lastSeen = {-1, -1, -1}; // indices for a, b, c
        int count = 0;
        
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            
            // Update last seen position for current character
            lastSeen[c - 'a'] = i;
            
            // Find the minimum of last seen positions (leftmost boundary)
            int minPos = Math.min(lastSeen[0], Math.min(lastSeen[1], lastSeen[2]));
            
            // If all characters have been seen (minPos != -1),
            // all substrings ending at i and starting at or before minPos are valid
            if (minPos != -1) {
                count += minPos + 1;
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    // Method 2: Sliding Window with HashMap (More Intuitive)
    public static int numberOfSubstringsHashMap(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() < 3) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        for (int left = 0; left < n; left++) {
            int[] charCount = new int[3]; // Count for a, b, c
            int distinctChars = 0;
            
            for (int right = left; right < n; right++) {
                char c = s.charAt(right);
                
                // If this character appears for the first time in current window
                if (charCount[c - 'a'] == 0) {
                    distinctChars++;
                }
                charCount[c - 'a']++;
                
                // If we have all three characters
                if (distinctChars == 3) {
                    // All substrings from current right to end are valid
                    count += n - right;
                    break; // No need to extend further from this left
                }
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    // Method 3: Brute Force (For Understanding)
    public static int numberOfSubstringsBruteForce(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() < 3) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        // Check all possible substrings
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            boolean hasA = false, hasB = false, hasC = false;
            
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                char c = s.charAt(j);
                
                if (c == 'a') hasA = true;
                else if (c == 'b') hasB = true;
                else if (c == 'c') hasC = true;
                
                // If all three characters are present
                if (hasA && hasB && hasC) {
                    // All substrings from i to k (k >= j) are valid
                    count += n - j;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    // Method 4: Alternative Optimized Approach
    public static int numberOfSubstringsAlternative(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() < 3) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int left = 0;
        int[] charCount = new int[3];
        
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            // Add current character to window
            charCount[s.charAt(right) - 'a']++;
            
            // Shrink window until we don't have all three characters
            while (charCount[0] > 0 && charCount[1] > 0 && charCount[2] > 0) {
                // All substrings starting from left to current right are valid
                count += s.length() - right;
                
                // Remove leftmost character and move left pointer
                charCount[s.charAt(left) - 'a']--;
                left++;
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Test cases
        String test1 = "abcabc";
        System.out.println("Test 'abcabc': " + numberOfSubstrings(test1)); // Expected: 10
        
        String test2 = "aaacb";
        System.out.println("Test 'aaacb': " + numberOfSubstrings(test2)); // Expected: 3
        
        String test3 = "abc";
        System.out.println("Test 'abc': " + numberOfSubstrings(test3)); // Expected: 1
        
        String test4 = "abacbc";
        System.out.println("Test 'abacbc': " + numberOfSubstrings(test4)); // Expected: 10
        
        // Compare different approaches
        System.out.println("\nComparison for 'abcabc':");
        System.out.println("Optimized: " + numberOfSubstrings(test1));
        System.out.println("HashMap: " + numberOfSubstringsHashMap(test1));
        System.out.println("Brute Force: " + numberOfSubstringsBruteForce(test1));
        System.out.println("Alternative: " + numberOfSubstringsAlternative(test1));
    }
}
```

🔥 HINTS:
1. Key insight: If substring [i,j] contains all three chars, then [i,k] for all k≥j also contains all three
2. Track the last seen position of each character as you iterate
3. The minimum of last seen positions gives you the leftmost valid starting point
4. Count = minPosition + 1 (all positions from 0 to minPosition can start valid substrings)

