🔴 PROBLEM #6: COUNT SUBSTRINGS WITH BALANCED PARENTHESES (MEDIUM)
===============================================================================

📋 PROBLEM STATEMENT:
Count the number of substrings that have balanced parentheses.
Input: "(()"
Output: 1
Explanation: Only "()" at indices 1-2 is balanced.

💡 INTUITION:
- A balanced string has equal opening and closing parentheses
- At any point, we shouldn't have more closing than opening brackets
- Use stack-like counting: increment for '(', decrement for ')'

🔍 APPROACH:
1. Brute Force: Check each substring for balance - O(n³)
2. Optimized: For each starting position, track balance while extending - O(n²)
3. Stack Simulation: Use counter instead of actual stack - O(n²)

⚡ COMPLEXITY:
Time: O(n²) where n is string length
Space: O(1)

💻 JAVA SOLUTION:
```java
public class CountBalancedParentheses {
    
    public static int countBalancedSubstrings(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        // Try all possible starting positions
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int balance = 0; // Counter for parentheses balance
            
            // Extend substring from position i
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                char c = s.charAt(j);
                
                if (c == '(') {
                    balance++;
                } else if (c == ')') {
                    balance--;
                }
                
                // If balance becomes negative, no valid substring possible
                if (balance < 0) {
                    break;
                }
                
                // If balance is 0 and we have at least one pair, it's balanced
                if (balance == 0 && j > i) {
                    count++;
                    // Optional: Print balanced substring
                    // System.out.println(s.substring(i, j + 1));
                }
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    // Enhanced version that handles any characters (not just parentheses)
    public static int countBalancedSubstringsEnhanced(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int count = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int balance = 0;
            boolean hasParentheses = false;
            
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                char c = s.charAt(j);
                
                if (c == '(') {
                    balance++;
                    hasParentheses = true;
                } else if (c == ')') {
                    balance--;
                    hasParentheses = true;
                }
                
                // Invalid if more closing than opening
                if (balance < 0) {
                    break;
                }
                
                // Valid balanced substring
                if (balance == 0 && hasParentheses) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    // Alternative: Using dynamic programming for optimization
    public static int countBalancedSubstringsDP(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.length() == 0) return 0;
        
        int n = s.length();
        int count = 0;
        
        // dp[i] represents the length of valid parentheses ending at index i
        int[] dp = new int[n];
        
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (s.charAt(i) == ')') {
                if (s.charAt(i - 1) == '(') {
                    // Case: ()
                    dp[i] = (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2] : 0) + 2;
                } else if (dp[i - 1] > 0) {
                    // Case: ))
                    int matchIndex = i - dp[i - 1] - 1;
                    if (matchIndex >= 0 && s.charAt(matchIndex) == '(') {
                        dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + 2 + (matchIndex > 0 ? dp[matchIndex - 1] : 0);
                    }
                }
            }
            
            if (dp[i] > 0) {
                // Count all valid substrings ending at position i
                for (int len = 2; len <= dp[i]; len += 2) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String test1 = "(()";
        System.out.println("Test '(()': " + countBalancedSubstrings(test1)); // Should be 1
        
        String test2 = "()()";
        System.out.println("Test '()()': " + countBalancedSubstrings(test2)); // Should be 3
        
        String test3 = "((()))";
        System.out.println("Test '((()))': " + countBalancedSubstrings(test3)); // Should be 4
        
        // Test enhanced version
        String test4 = "a(b)c(d)e";
        System.out.println("Enhanced 'a(b)c(d)e': " + countBalancedSubstringsEnhanced(test4));
    }
}
```

🔥 HINTS:
1. Use a counter instead of stack for space efficiency
2. Balance < 0 means invalid, break immediately
3. Balance = 0 means potentially valid (if we've seen parentheses)
4. Only count when balance returns to 0 and length > 0

